  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider $c$-level curves for any real value of $c.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 3-2x-3y & =c \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1-\\frac{c}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for any real value of $c$, the right-hand side of an equation is the equation of a line. As a result, we obtain a family of lines with slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14152724/assets/7ab95ed367277627d5baf535db74994d7d02cd41deae939d76ed08e22799ff40.png)",
